1. `option_bag_junk_drawer` — `describeCheckout`

[src/petty.ts:10](/Users/andrew/dev/crimes/evals/fixtures/01-messy-ts-app/src/petty.ts:10) reads six unrelated properties from one broad bag. Although `CheckoutOptions` is named, it still mixes plan, actor, location, payment, and retry state.

Refactor into cohesive records and destructure the exact dependencies:

```ts
interface CheckoutDescription {
  checkout: {
    plan: string;
    status: string;
    shouldRetry: boolean;
  };
  customer: {
    role: string;
    region: string;
  };
  payment: {
    currency: string;
  };
}

export function describeCheckout({
  checkout,
  customer,
  payment,
}: CheckoutDescription): string {
  return [
    checkout.plan,
    customer.role,
    customer.region,
    payment.currency,
    checkout.status,
    String(checkout.shouldRetry),
  ].join(":");
}
```

Today, an agent could add another vaguely relevant property to the junk drawer, omit it at a caller, or place it incorrectly in the positional serialized output. The narrower groups make ownership and required fields explicit.

2. `return_shape_roulette` — `resolvePaymentResult`

[src/petty.ts:21](/Users/andrew/dev/crimes/evals/fixtures/01-messy-ts-app/src/petty.ts:21) returns three shapes with zero key overlap in some branches and has no explicit return type.

Introduce a discriminated union:

```ts
type PaymentResult =
  | {
      kind: "error";
      code: string;
      message: string;
    }
  | {
      kind: "redirect";
      url: string;
      permanent: boolean;
    }
  | {
      kind: "success";
      value: string;
      cached: boolean;
    };

export function resolvePaymentResult(input: PaymentInput): PaymentResult {
  if (input.error) {
    return {
      kind: "error",
      code: input.code ?? "unknown",
      message: input.message ?? "Payment failed",
    };
  }

  if (input.redirect) {
    return {
      kind: "redirect",
      url: input.url ?? "",
      permanent: false,
    };
  }

  return {
    kind: "success",
    value: input.value ?? "",
    cached: false,
  };
}
```

Callers must now narrow on `result.kind`. Today, an agent might assume every result has `ok`, access `url` without checking the branch, or introduce a fourth undocumented shape. Ideally, make `PaymentInput` a discriminated union too, preventing contradictory inputs such as both `error` and `redirect`.

3. `negative_flag_maze` — `canRunBilling`

[src/petty.ts:34](/Users/andrew/dev/crimes/evals/fixtures/01-messy-ts-app/src/petty.ts:34) combines `!disableBilling && !skipRetry`. Rename both inputs positively and return the predicate directly:

```ts
export function canRunBilling(
  isBillingEnabled: boolean,
  isRetryEnabled: boolean,
): boolean {
  return isBillingEnabled && isRetryEnabled;
}
```

Call sites must invert their existing negative values during migration:

```ts
canRunBilling(!disableBilling, !skipRetry);
```

Today, an agent adding an exception could easily negate only one flag, change `&&` to `||`, or misread “not skipped” as “skip.” Positive names remove that mental inversion and also resolve the accompanying `boolean_naming_drift` finding.

Before changing behavior, add focused tests—especially all four truth-table combinations for `canRunBilling` and exhaustive branch tests for `PaymentResult`. The context report found no likely tests for this file.
